# the aarch64 calling conventions: aapcs64, apple's darwinpcs, windows'
# aapcs64win and arm64ec
#
# implemented to the procedure call standard for the arm 64-bit architecture,
# "parameter passing" (aapcs64 2025q4, stages a to c), to apple's "writing
# arm64 code for apple platforms", which darwinpcs states as differences from
# it, to microsoft's "overview of arm64 abi conventions", which states
# aapcs64win the same way, and to microsoft's "overview of arm64ec abi
# conventions", which states arm64ec over both arm64's and x64's. the one
# classifier reads every row: the
# registers, the word, the pairing and the packing are the row's, and the
# unnamed rule is its variadic model, so no row has code of its own.
#
# a value is placed by the first rule that takes it:
# - a value the call passes by reference takes a general register or a slot
#   for its copy's address
# - a float or a short vector of 8 or 16 bytes takes the next vector register
#   (c.1), and with none left goes on the stack (c.4 to c.6). a vector of any
#   other size is no type either standard states, and travels as the row's
#   mirl-defined `defined` row says
# - a homogeneous aggregate, of one to four members of one float format or of
#   one short vector size, takes as many consecutive vector registers when
#   they are all free (c.2), and otherwise none is left (c.3) and it goes on
#   the stack whole
# - a composite wider than 16 bytes travels by reference (b.4)
# - an integer, a pointer or a composite of up to 16 bytes takes one or two
#   general registers, the second starting at an even register when the
#   value's alignment is 16 and the row pairs even (c.9 to c.12). with too few
#   left, none is left (c.13) and it goes on the stack whole
# - on the stack a value starts at its alignment, at least 8 where the row
#   packs by slot (c.14 to c.17), and a composite's size is rounded up to 8
#   (b.5)
# - under darwinpcs an unnamed value goes on the stack in 8 byte slots
# - under aapcs64win every argument of a variadic call, named or not, takes
#   the words of one area whose first 64 bytes x0 to x7 hold, at its
#   alignment, and no vector register
# - under arm64ec every argument of a variadic call, named or not, takes one
#   word as x64 places it, in x0 to x3 and then in 8 byte stack slots, a
#   composite of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes in its word and any other composite or
#   value wider than a word by reference
# results are placed as a first named argument would be, in the result
# registers, and a result that would travel by reference makes its address
# travel in x8, which is no argument register. a call may be a tail call when
# it passes nothing on the stack and no result by reference.

